[[The weir at dusk]]

:: The weir at dusk

The boat was the wardens' long boat, but there was only one man in it, and it was Captain Marsh, alone, poling against the pull of the weir with his hat gone and his coat black with spray.

The pole in Marsh's hands caught on something under the surface and would not come free, and the boat swung broadside to the weir, and the weir took it.

She saw him go over. She stood in the roar of it and counted to two hundred, because nobody came up from the weir at Hollin Reach, not in forty years, not ever.

[[The toll at dawn]]

:: The toll at dawn

In the morning the wardens' long boat was tied up at the far jetty, and Captain Marsh stood in the stern with one boot on the gunwale, the way he always stood, dry, with his hat on.

"Toll," he said.
